coreletd for Debian
===================

The service is installed but deliberately NOT enabled
-----------------------------------------------------

Starting this daemon puts a 22 dBm PA on the air. Which band you may transmit
on is a legal question, and transmitting into an unfitted antenna connector
will damage the radio, so installing the package never starts it. Review the
configuration first:

    sudoedit /etc/coreletd/coreletd.ini

`lora_freq` in `[radio]` is required and has no built-in default; the shipped
file leaves it unset. Set the plan for your region, fit an antenna, then:

    sudo systemctl enable --now coreletd
    journalctl -u coreletd -f

Upgrading the package restarts the daemon if you enabled it, and leaves it
alone if you did not.

Before the first start
----------------------

1. Enable SPI. The AIO v2 puts the SX1262 on SPI1, so add to
   /boot/firmware/config.txt and reboot:

       dtparam=spi=on
       dtoverlay=spi1-1cs

   Confirm /dev/spidev1.0 exists afterwards. The unit has
   ConditionPathExists=/dev/spidev1.0 and will refuse to start without it.

2. Turn on the LoRa power rail (GPIO16). The daemon does not switch it -- it
   is board-level state owned by whatever manages the uConsole's power. Until
   it is high the SX1262 is unpowered; the daemon starts anyway and connects
   to the radio on its next retry (`lora_retry_interval` in `[hardware]`, 10
   seconds).

3. Stop meshtasticd if it is installed -- it holds the same GPIO lines and
   coreletd will fail with EBUSY.

The package creates a system user `coreletd` and a system group of the same
name. The udev rules in /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/99-coreletd.rules grant that
group access to the SPI bus and the GPIO character devices. Adding yourself
to `coreletd` lets you run the daemon by hand for debugging:

    sudo adduser "$USER" coreletd     # log out and back in

State, and what purging does not remove
---------------------------------------

On first start the daemon generates an Ed25519 identity and writes it to
/var/lib/coreletd/identity (mode 0600). That file *is* your node: back it up.
Deleting it gives you a new public key and every contact will see you as a
stranger.

For that reason purging the package leaves /var/lib/coreletd and the
`coreletd` user and group in place. Remove them by hand if you really mean
to discard the node identity:

    sudo rm -rf /var/lib/coreletd
    sudo deluser coreletd
    sudo delgroup coreletd

Everything in /var/lib/coreletd is private to the `coreletd` user: the
directory is 0700 and every file in it 0600 -- the identity, the channel
secrets in `channels`, and messages waiting for an app in `messages`.
Membership of the `coreletd` group grants the socket and the radio, not the
state, so read or back it up with sudo.

The directory is the whole node and a copy of it is a complete backup. Each
file is replaced atomically, so it can be taken while the service runs:

    sudo tar -C /var/lib -czf coreletd-backup.tar.gz coreletd

The archive holds the private key; keep it private too. Restore with the
service stopped, since a running daemon writes its own state over the
restored files at its next save, and leave the files owned by `coreletd`:

    sudo systemctl stop coreletd
    sudo tar -C /var/lib -xzf coreletd-backup.tar.gz
    sudo chown -R coreletd:coreletd /var/lib/coreletd
    sudo systemctl start coreletd

If the identity is missing while the other files are present, the daemon
refuses to start rather than generate a new node on top of them.

Companion protocol access
-------------------------

The companion protocol has no authentication at all. It therefore defaults to
/run/coreletd/companion.sock, owned by coreletd:coreletd with mode 0660. Add a
local client's user to the coreletd group, then log out and back in:

    sudo adduser "$USER" coreletd

Set `companion_interface = tcp` for TCP-only clients. Selecting TCP is the
explicit network opt-in; it defaults to 127.0.0.1. Prefer an SSH tunnel or
WireGuard to widening `companion_bind` for direct network exposure.

